<Project>

  <!--
    Package metadata shared by all three packages. Kept here rather than repeated in each csproj so
    the three cannot drift apart on a feed, and matched to Optimizely.Performance.DotNetCounters,
    which they always ship alongside.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Authors>Optimizely</Authors>
    <Company>Optimizely</Company>
    <Version>1.0.0</Version>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/optimizely</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/optimizely/performance-counters</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageTags>optimizely episerver performance counters monitoring application-insights telemetry</PackageTags>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The Optimizely versions each target framework is built and tested against, and the minimums the
    published packages declare.

    Pinned rather than floating (11.*, 12.*, ...). A floating reference makes the minimum version in
    the shipped .nuspec whatever happened to be latest on the machine that built it, so the same
    commit produces different packages on different days - and it already had the CMS and Commerce
    packages declaring different minimum EPiServer.Framework versions from one build.

    These are also the versions the test project compiles against, so what the tests prove is what
    the packages promise. Raising one is a deliberate change to the support matrix; see
    OptimizelyVersionDetector for the target-framework side of the same map.

    Each is the LOWEST version that works, not the newest available. A NuGet version is a floor
    rather than a pin, so the number chosen here is the oldest Optimizely a site may be running and
    still install these packages; a site that is ahead is unaffected either way. Partners and
    customers routinely run some way behind current, and a floor set to whatever was newest on the
    day locks those sites out of a diagnostic package for no benefit to anyone. So each of these is
    pushed down until something concrete stops it, and what stopped it is recorded below.

    Compiling against the floor is what makes the floor real, which is why these are the versions the
    projects build against and not just the numbers in the .nuspec. Optimizely gives every release a
    new AssemblyVersion - CMS 12.10.0 ships 12.10.0.0, 12.24.1 ships 12.24.1.0 - so the direction
    matters: an assembly compiled against the floor binds against everything above it, on .NET Core
    because the runtime resolves the higher version it finds, and on net472 through the binding
    redirects a CMS 11 site already has to carry for Optimizely's own assemblies. Compiling against
    the newest and declaring that as the minimum, which is what this used to do, gets the same
    protection only by refusing to install at all.

    Building at the floor does mean the default test run no longer exercises current Optimizely, so
    it is run at both ends. See "Testing at the ceiling" in docs/SMOKE_TEST.md.

    The trade is that no Optimizely API introduced after a floor is available to us. Nothing here
    needs one - the instrumented surface is IContentLoader, IContentRepository,
    ISynchronizedObjectInstanceCache, IOrderRepository and IEventPublisher, all long-standing - but
    reaching for something newer means raising the floor with it, deliberately.

    One warning survives all this and is expected. EPiServer.CMS.AspNetCore pins its sibling
    EPiServer.CMS.Core to its own exact version, while Commerce asks for both as a range and so
    settles AspNetCore on the range floor; the CMS minimum DotNetCounters imposes then lifts CMS.Core
    past that pin, which is NU1608. It shows up on a project that references nothing but these
    packages and not on a real site, where the site's own CMS.AspNetCore reference lifts it to match.
    Closing it means lowering the minimums in DotNetCounters - or raising the Commerce floor to a
    release whose own CMS floor already clears 12.10.0, which trades reach for a quiet log and is not
    the trade this file is making.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- V11: CMS 11 / Commerce 13, net472.
         CMS 11 held up by Commerce 13.0.0, which asks for EPiServer.Framework [11.11.1, 12.0.0).
         Nothing of ours needs it: the CMS package alone still builds against 11.1.0. One number
         rather than two is the only reason the CMS package does not go lower.
         Commerce 13 is at the bottom of its major, and unusually free of consequence - every
         13.x ships the same frozen AssemblyVersion 3.0.30930.28736, so no binding redirect is
         involved anywhere in the range. -->
    <OptimizelyV11Version>11.11.1</OptimizelyV11Version>
    <CommerceV13Version>13.0.0</CommerceV13Version>

    <!-- V12: CMS 12 / Commerce 14, net6.0-net9.0.
         12.10.0 is a hard floor and not our choice: Optimizely.Performance.DotNetCounters 1.0.0 -
         the dependency these packages always ship beside - declares EPiServer.CMS.Core and
         EPiServer.Framework >= 12.10.0 in its net6.0 group, which every consumer inherits through
         Core. 12.5.0 restores as an NU1605 downgrade and 12.0.3 as an NU1107 conflict, but only
         the first of those is ours to fix, by lowering the minimums in DotNetCounters.
         Commerce 14.5.0 is the oldest that ships a net6.0 assembly at all, so it is the floor for
         a band that starts at net6.0. It names CMS 12.4.0, below ours, so it constrains nothing.
         One value covers net6.0 through net9.0. There used to be two, because Commerce 14.46
         dropped net6.0 and net7.0 - an upper bound, which stops mattering once these are floors. -->
    <OptimizelyV12Version>12.10.0</OptimizelyV12Version>
    <CommerceV14Version>14.5.0</CommerceV14Version>

    <!-- V13: CMS 13 / Commerce 15, net10.0.
         13.0.2 rather than 13.0.0, which is what DotNetCounters asks for and is the bottom of the
         major. Commerce 15.0.0 requires CMS 13.0.2, and Optimizely's inner packages pin each other
         exactly - EPiServer.Cms.Core 13.0.2 -> EPiServer 13.0.2 -> EPiServer.Events (= 13.0.2) - so
         a CMS floor even one patch below the one Commerce names is an NU1107 conflict rather than a
         downgrade warning. The two have to move together. -->
    <OptimizelyV13Version>13.0.2</OptimizelyV13Version>
    <CommerceV15Version>15.0.0</CommerceV15Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
